    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF",
    "problem": "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $7{x}^{3}$ and $42{x}^{2}$ is $7{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}(x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$",
